---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---

<Layout title="System Release Version Log" description="System Release Version Log — Hunter Myers">
  <nav class="breadcrumb">
    <a href={`${base}/professional-projects/`}>&larr; Professional Projects</a>
  </nav>

  <section class="section">
    <h1>System Release Version Log</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">Garmin International &middot; 2017 – 2020</p>
  </section>

  <div class="project-body">

    <section class="project-section">
      <h2>The Problem</h2>
      <p>
        I joined the "Airframe" team in 2017, which is responsible for generating software deliverables that
        are installed on customer aircraft in forward-fit installations of Garmin Integrated Flight Deck (GIFD) products.
        A GIFD contains multiple pieces of hardware, called Line Replaceable Units (LRUs), and each LRU has
        its own software development team, release schedule, QA, etc.  For a GIFD product to work, all LRUs must be
        compatible with each other.  At the time I joined, the Aviation segment was experiencing significant growth,
        both in number of customer aircraft models and in the number of distinct LRUs.  LRU Software schedules were handled
        on an excel spreadsheet, which could not scale with the business.  
      </p>
      <p>
        Along with scaling issues, it was becoming extremely difficult and time-consuming for Airframe Leads, who are responsible
        for packaging all configuration and LRU software together, to know which versions to use in their build tools.  Airframe Leads
        would look at the spreadsheet and manually enter values in their build tool, only to often find out the spreadsheet was out of
        date, or they didn't know where to find the actual software binaries in the in-house artifact manager.  This led to quality
        escapes because the right versions were not being tested.
      </p>
      <figure class="diagram-figure">
        <img
          src={`${base}/diagrams/sysrel_version_log_before.drawio.svg`}
          alt="State of the system before the Version Log"
          class="diagram"
          data-full={`${base}/diagrams/sysrel_version_log_before.drawio.svg`}
        />
        <figcaption>In the beginning, there was chaos.</figcaption>
        <span class="diagram-hint">Click to enlarge</span>
      </figure>
      <p>A more scalable approach was needed, which would:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Implement a coordinated "System Release" model for all LRU software to be ready together</li>
        <li>Provide a one-page dashboard of the status and health of each System Release during the development cycle</li>
        <li>Reduce quality escapes by providing accurate LRU version information to Airframe Leads</li>
      </ul>
      
    </section>

    <section class="project-section">
      <h2>Design Decisions &amp; Tradeoffs</h2>
      <p>
        From prior experience I felt a web app would be the best way to provide real-time information to all stakeholders.  A single
        page could show all versions of software, for all current branches, along with the latest developmental version.  The concept was
        simple, but the actual data flow was less straightforward.  How would the data be sourced, and how would I know it was accurate?
        How would it integrate into Airframe Lead build tools?  Which data would be entered manually, and where could automation help?
        And which stakeholders would I need to bring on board to make everything happen?
      </p>
      <p>
        The actual source of truth for each LRU was the LRU team itself.  I did not believe a fully automated way to capture the data was
        achievable, so my design incorporated crowdsourcing of data from each LRU team lead.  For that to work, they would need to use the tool.  Each lead reported to a manager; those were the decision makers
        when it came to process changes like what I was proposing.  So I started by creating a POC of the design - database, API, and web
        page - and demoed it to the software managers and VP.  They agreed with the approach, so I was able to move forward.
      </p>
      <p>
        The initial version of the SysRel Version Log was fully manual, but I knew (from working as an Airframe Lead myself) where automation
        would be most beneficial, and solidified a roadmap for that.  A new role, the Release Coordinator, was created to plan and schedule releases.
        The web page had special controls accessible only to authorized Release Coordinators.  At this point the Version Log went live.
      </p>
      <figure class="diagram-figure">
        <img
          src={`${base}/diagrams/sysrel_version_log_mvp.drawio.svg`}
          alt="MVP design of the Version Log"
          class="diagram"
          data-full={`${base}/diagrams/sysrel_version_log_mvp.drawio.svg`}
        />
        <figcaption>Version Log MVP: a scalable source of truth.</figcaption>
        <span class="diagram-hint">Click to enlarge</span>
      </figure>
      <p>
        The first step was to automatically update the Log every time a developmental version of software was created.  Ideally, there would
        be a standardized CI/CD pipeline I could hook into.  I had multiple discussions with various teams, and found there really was no
        standard.  I did not believe creating and implementing a standard was going to be achievable due to the loosely organized nature of
        the organization and a culture that emphasized organic growth over top-down processes.  So I came up with a different strategy: a
        process that would crawl through the artifact registry and "discover" new versions of LRU binaries.  This required teams to consistently
        tag their software, which turned out to be a challenge.  I got the standard approved at the VP level and socialized it, and had to
        act as Tag Police for a time to ensure compliance.
      </p>
      <figure class="diagram-figure">
        <img
          src={`${base}/diagrams/sysrel_version_log_discovery.drawio.svg`}
          alt="Version discovery process"
          class="diagram"
          data-full={`${base}/diagrams/sysrel_version_log_discovery.drawio.svg`}
        />
        <figcaption>Dev binary discovery: order from chaos.</figcaption>
        <span class="diagram-hint">Click to enlarge</span>
      </figure>
      <p>
        Automatic updates enabled the Version Log to be integrated with Airframe Lead build tools - this was the real payoff.  Here again the policy of organic growth
        was problematic because every Airframe Lead had custom Python scripts that were the entry point to the build process, and the versions
        were defined in those scripts.  There was no standardization, so I could not rely on inputs to the scripts.  I created a new component of the 
        build tool that would scan the completed software deliverable, extract which versions were being used, compare those against the Version Log, and 
        alert the Airframe Lead if their deliverable was outdated in any way.
      </p>
      <figure class="diagram-figure">
        <img
          src={`${base}/diagrams/sysrel_version_log_payoff.drawio.svg`}
          alt="Full system payoff diagram"
          class="diagram"
          data-full={`${base}/diagrams/sysrel_version_log_payoff.drawio.svg`}
        />
        <figcaption>The payoff: increased delivery speed, reduced quality escapes.</figcaption>
        <span class="diagram-hint">Click to enlarge</span>
      </figure>
      <p>
        Ultimately the individual python scripts were replaced with an Electron-based GUI which enabled direct population from the Version Log.
        The Version Log API powered that new tool, and I made strong contributions in design and testing.
      </p>





    </section>

    <section class="project-section">
      <h2>Outcome</h2>
      <p>
        The Version Log became critical to Garmin's ability to manage software releases.  Soon after
        adoption, the number of supported branches tripled.  This simply would not have been
        manageable without the scalability offered by the Version Log.
      </p>
      <p>
        The various automated features unburdened Airframe Leads from tedious and error-prone data gathering.
        This freed up time for implementation, and reduced quality escapes since alerts were generated for
        any compatibility issues.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Version Log was refactored after my time at Garmin, but still exists and continues to serve the
        Aviation segment.
      </p>
    </section>

  </div>

  <div id="lightbox" class="lightbox" aria-hidden="true">
    <img id="lightbox-img" src="" alt="" class="lightbox-img" />
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .breadcrumb {
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
  }

  .project-body {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 2.5rem;
  }

  .project-section h2 {
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
    padding-bottom: 0.5rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .project-section p,
  .project-section li {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  .project-section ul {
    margin: 0.5rem 0 0 1.25rem;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.3rem;
  }

  .diagram-group {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 1.5rem;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .diagram-figure {
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 1.5rem;
    text-align: center;
  }

  .diagram {
    max-width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    cursor: zoom-in;
    transition: opacity 0.15s;
  }

  .diagram:hover {
    opacity: 0.85;
  }

  figcaption {
    margin-top: 1rem;
    font-size: 1rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--text);
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent);
    padding-left: 0.75rem;
    text-align: left;
  }

  .diagram-hint {
    display: block;
    margin-top: 0.25rem;
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    opacity: 0.6;
  }

  .lightbox {
    display: none;
    position: fixed;
    inset: 0;
    background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.85);
    z-index: 1000;
    cursor: zoom-out;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    padding: 2rem;
  }

  .lightbox.open {
    display: flex;
  }

  .lightbox-img {
    max-width: 100%;
    max-height: 100%;
    object-fit: contain;
    border-radius: 4px;
  }
</style>

<script>
  const lightbox = document.getElementById('lightbox') as HTMLElement;
  const lightboxImg = document.getElementById('lightbox-img') as HTMLImageElement;

  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLImageElement>('.diagram').forEach(diagram => {
    diagram.addEventListener('click', () => {
      lightboxImg.src = diagram.dataset.full ?? diagram.src;
      lightboxImg.alt = diagram.alt;
      lightbox.classList.add('open');
      lightbox.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'false');
    });
  });

  lightbox.addEventListener('click', () => {
    lightbox.classList.remove('open');
    lightbox.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');
  });

  document.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {
    if (e.key === 'Escape') {
      lightbox.classList.remove('open');
      lightbox.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');
    }
  });
</script>
